# Source: HoareLogic.pdf, section "Exercises", "A loop that adds two in each iteration".
id: adds-two
title: A loop that adds two in each iteration
topic: Loops
level: 2
source: HoareLogic.pdf, Exercises, "A loop that adds two in each iteration"
tags: while, invariant, bounds, sequence

task:
  Find a loop invariant, and prove that the triple is valid using the rule
  for sequential composition, the assignment rule and the loop rule with
  consequence. Hint: the invariant has to relate the value of $s$ with the
  value of $i$.

pre: n >= 0
post: s = 2 * n

program:
  {P}
  {R4}
  i := 0;            // i1
  {R3}
  s := 0;            // i2
  {I}
  while i < n do {
    {I && i < n}
    {R2}
    i := i + 1;      // b1
    {R1}
    s := s + 2       // b2
    {I}
  }
  {I && !(i < n)}
  {Q}

verdict: valid

step: Find a candidate invariant
rule: WHILE-CONSEQUENCE
focus: while
text:
  At the loop head the values are $(i, s) = (0, 0), (1, 2), (2, 4), ...$,
  so $s = 2 * i$. We obtain the same formula by replacing $n$ by the
  progress variable $i$ in the postcondition. We also add the bounds of
  $i$, which go from $0$ to $n$.
set: I = s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n

step: Establishment, through the initialisation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, WP
focus: i1, i2
text:
  The loop is preceded by two assignments, so establishment is the triple
  $\{P\}$ `i := 0; s := 0` $\{I\}$. We compute its weakest precondition.
calc:
  wp(s := 0, I)
  = 0 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n
set: R3 = 0 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n
calc:
  wp(i := 0, R3)
  = 0 = 2 * 0 && 0 <= 0 <= n
  = 0 <= n
set: R4 = 0 <= n
vc: n >= 0 ==> 0 <= n

step: Preservation
rule: ASSIGN, SEQ, CONSEQUENCE
focus: b1, b2
calc:
  wp(s := s + 2, I)
  = s + 2 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n
set: R1 = s + 2 = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n
calc:
  wp(i := i + 1, R1)
  = s + 2 = 2 * (i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
set: R2 = s + 2 = 2 * (i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
vc: s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> s + 2 = 2 * (i + 1) && 0 <= i + 1 <= n
why:
  From $s = 2 * i$ we get $s + 2 = 2 * i + 2 = 2 * (i + 1)$. From
  $0 <= i$ we get $0 <= i + 1$, and from $i < n$ we get $i + 1 <= n$.

step: The postcondition at the exit
rule: CONSEQUENCE, LOGIC
vc: s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n && !(i < n) ==> s = 2 * n
why:
  $!(i < n)$ is $i >= n$. Together with $i <= n$ it gives $i = n$, and
  then $s = 2 * i$ becomes $s = 2 * n$.
result: The triple is valid.

step: Why the bounds are needed
rule: LOGIC
text:
  The reference document asks why $s = 2 * i$ alone is not enough. It is
  an invariant: it holds at the start and it is preserved. But it fails the
  exit check, since without $i <= n$ the negated condition only gives
  $i >= n$.
vc: s = 2 * i && !(i < n) ==> s = 2 * n
status: fails
counterexample: n = 1, i = 2, s = 4
why:
  The state $n = 1$, $i = 2$, $s = 4$ satisfies $s = 2 * i$ and $i >= n$,
  but $s != 2 * n$. The bound $i <= n$ rules this state out.

step: Termination
rule: WHILE-TOTAL
text:
  The variable $i$ increases towards $n$, so we take the variant $n - i$.
vc: s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n ==> n - i >= 0
vc: {s = 2 * i && 0 <= i <= n && i < n && n - i = E0} i := i + 1; s := s + 2 {n - i < E0}
why:
  After the body, $n - (i + 1) = E0 - 1 < E0$.
